Got it! You're planning to store the .crt and .key contents as GitLab CI/CD variables of type file — that’s a secure and Git-safe approach.

Let me walk you through how to wire that into your Terraform flow, step-by-step:

✅ Step-by-Step Guide: Upload .crt and .key to Azure Key Vault via GitLab CI/CD
🔐 Step 1: Define GitLab CI/CD Variables
In GitLab > Settings > CI/CD > Variables:

Add a file-type variable for each secret:

Key	Type	Value
CLIENT_CRT_FILE	File	Paste contents of .crt
CLIENT_KEY_FILE	File	Paste contents of .key

GitLab stores these as temporary files during the pipeline.

🧪 Step 2: Terraform Code to Upload from GitLab CI/CD Runtime
Update your Terraform to read the temporary files provided by GitLab:

hcl
Copy
Edit
# Read environment variables (path to temp files created by GitLab)
locals {
  client_crt = filebase64(var.client_crt_path)
  client_key = filebase64(var.client_key_path)
}

variable "client_crt_path" {
  description = "Path to the .crt file (from GitLab file variable)"
  type        = string
}

variable "client_key_path" {
  description = "Path to the .key file (from GitLab file variable)"
  type        = string
}

resource "azurerm_key_vault_secret" "client_crt" {
  name         = "client-crt"
  value        = local.client_crt
  key_vault_id = azurerm_key_vault.myvault.id
}

resource "azurerm_key_vault_secret" "client_key" {
  name         = "client-key"
  value        = local.client_key
  key_vault_id = azurerm_key_vault.myvault.id
}
🧰 Step 3: GitLab .gitlab-ci.yml Setup
Use the file variables in the job and pass their paths to Terraform:

yaml
Copy
Edit
upload_certs:
  stage: deploy
  script:
    - echo "Uploading certs to Azure Key Vault"
    - terraform init
    - terraform apply -auto-approve \
        -var="client_crt_path=$CLIENT_CRT_FILE" \
        -var="client_key_path=$CLIENT_KEY_FILE"
  variables:
    TF_VAR_client_crt_path: $CLIENT_CRT_FILE
    TF_VAR_client_key_path: $CLIENT_KEY_FILE
CLIENT_CRT_FILE and CLIENT_KEY_FILE are automatically created as file paths when using file-type GitLab variables.

🔒 Security Notes
GitLab masks file variables — they aren’t exposed in logs.

The files are deleted after the job.

Only Terraform has access to them during the job.

The actual contents never touch your repo.
